## Project layout

- `{{ django_project_slug }}/` — Django settings, URL root, WSGI/ASGI entry points{% if include_celery %}, and the Celery app{% endif %}.
- `apps/{{ initial_app_name }}/` — the first Django app: models, serializers, views, urls,
  tests. New apps follow the same `apps/<name>/` layout and are added to `INSTALLED_APPS`
  as `apps.<name>`.
- Database: **{{ database }}**{% if db_is_server %}. The `db` service runs from
  `docker-compose.yml`, which you bring up yourself from inside the devcontainer with its
  in-container Docker daemon (`docker compose up -d`). It publishes on `127.0.0.1:{{ db_port }}`;
  `DATABASE_URL` in `.env` (copy it from `.env.example`) already points Django at
  `localhost:{{ db_port }}`{% endif %}.
{% if include_celery %}
- Background tasks run through Celery with Redis as broker/result backend. Redis is the
  `redis` service in `docker-compose.yml` (same `docker compose up -d`), published on
  `127.0.0.1:6379`.
{% endif %}

## Running things

- `uv run python manage.py runserver` — dev server.
- `uv run python manage.py makemigrations` / `migrate` — schema changes.
- `uv run pytest` — test suite (uses SQLite automatically unless `DATABASE_URL` is set, so it
  runs without any external service).
{% if needs_service_stack %}
- `docker compose up -d` / `docker compose down` — start/stop the local
  {% if db_is_server %}{{ database }}{% endif %}{% if db_is_server and include_celery %} and {% endif %}{% if include_celery %}Redis{% endif %} container{% if db_is_server and include_celery %}s{% endif %}. This uses the devcontainer's own in-container Docker daemon; the containers are not on the host and are reached at `localhost`.
{% endif %}
